{
  "agentId": "opposition_research",
  "shape": "background",
  "placeholder": false,
  "note": "CORE CHECKS. The inputs are synthetic, written to make the sweep exercisable rather than drawn from a real dispatch or a real conversation, but the checked cases test the agent: each carries a condition and handledWhen for its main job, its top guardrail or a fallback path, written from the agent's own prompt and confirmed passing on main in a no-change sweep. A verdict over the unchecked cases is still a claim about the pipeline, and here that is two of the three cases CI walks: only partisan-race-with-incumbent held still between identical runs. EIGHT cases, and DEFAULT_JUDGE_CONFIG.gates.minCases is 5. CI walks only the first config.background.maxCases of them (3 today), under that floor, so a CI verdict over this list comes back gated: directional rather than conclusive. Eight is one clean baseline plus seven single-axis variations, which is what one change can author honestly. {judgeRaceId} and {judgeUserEmail} are not a value: they are substituted at dispatch time from the identifiers judgeIdentifiers.ts resolves once per sweep, and assertNoPlaceholders refuses the whole list before the first dispatch if a sweep did not supply one. They are not literals because the slug is derived from the sweep and the race id is read live from BallotReady's data. The race and the candidate identity are therefore the two axes this list cannot vary, and neither costs it anything. The manifest calls race_id a trace and idempotency identifier the agent does not reason over. user_email is only matched against campaign_strategy_context.candidates[].email to mark is_user and exclude the candidate from their own opponent list, so the same token appears twice — once as the param and once inside the roster row it has to match — which is why substitution recurses rather than rewriting top-level params. It is a token rather than a literal address for a second reason as well: this file is public and an authored address would be a plausible-looking one. Varied along: the roster, which is this agent's only input besides web search — three-way, head-to-head, seven filed, unopposed, and a count of four against an empty candidates array, which is the 'incomplete and lagging' state the schema warns about and the case where every opponent has to come from the web; whether the candidate's own row is in the roster at all, since with no email match is_user falls back to a fuzzy full_name match and getting it wrong means the candidate is filed as their own opponent; partisan against nonpartisan, where party labels are the contest in one and registration noise in the other, plus an incumbent to mark; and a race whose real filed field is the primary stage while the general roster is still empty. People are fictional. The candidate keeps the house-style 'Test Candidate', but roster opponents carry plain invented names because the instruction drops 'Test' rows as junk; this file is public, so no real person is named and every URL is under example.org. States, cities, office titles and L2 voter file column names are real.",
